1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,427
Orchard Core protects sign-in cookies, anti-forgery tokens and saved credentials with encryption keys.

2
00:00:07,427 --> 00:00:12,188
On one server they sit in a local folder; several servers must share them.

3
00:00:12,188 --> 00:00:15,997
You will see where the keys live, and how to store them in

4
00:00:15,997 --> 00:00:18,020
Azure Blob Storage or Redis.

5
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:27,493
ASP.NET Core Data Protection protects the values that a site hands to the

6
00:00:27,493 --> 00:00:29,529
browser or keeps for later.

7
00:00:29,529 --> 00:00:35,760
Orchard Core uses it for authentication cookies, anti-forgery tokens, saved credentials such as

8
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,412
an SMTP password, and temporary data.

9
00:00:38,412 --> 00:00:40,880
User passwords are not part of it.

10
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:45,418
By default, each tenant has its own key ring, the set of its

11
00:00:45,418 --> 00:00:50,699
keys, in a folder named DataProtection-Keys inside the tenant's folder under App_Data.

12
00:00:50,699 --> 00:00:56,783
The application name is the tenant name, so one tenant cannot decrypt what another tenant encrypted.

13
00:00:56,783 --> 00:00:58,620
Nothing needs configuring.

14
00:00:59,370 --> 00:01:02,481
The local folder breaks in a load-balanced site.

15
00:01:02,481 --> 00:01:07,435
Each server has its own keys, so a cookie issued by one server fails on another.

16
00:01:07,435 --> 00:01:11,699
A folder that does not survive a restart or a redeployment loses the

17
00:01:11,699 --> 00:01:15,040
keys, which invalidates existing cookies and tokens.

18
00:01:15,790 --> 00:01:17,895
The keys also guard stored data.

19
00:01:17,895 --> 00:01:22,381
Secrets and saved credentials are encrypted with the keys of the tenant, so

20
00:01:22,381 --> 00:01:25,150
a database backup alone cannot decrypt them.

21
00:01:25,150 --> 00:01:28,750
The key ring needs durable storage and a backup of its own.

22
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:36,810
You can keep the key ring of each tenant in three places:

23
00:01:36,810 --> 00:01:40,853
the local folder, an Azure Blob Storage container, or Redis.

24
00:01:40,853 --> 00:01:43,610
Two features provide the shared places:

25
00:01:43,610 --> 00:01:49,124
Distributed Data Protection (Azure Storage) and Distributed Data Protection (Redis).

26
00:01:49,124 --> 00:01:51,330
Enable the one for your store.

27
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:56,089
A feature replaces the local folder only when its settings exist.

28
00:01:56,089 --> 00:02:00,833
When a feature is enabled without settings, the tenant keeps the local folder,

29
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:03,600
and the feature writes an entry to the log.

30
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:10,848
Features are managed under Tools, Features.

31
00:02:10,848 --> 00:02:13,195
Let's search for data protection.

32
00:02:13,195 --> 00:02:16,565
Two features match, both in the Security category:

33
00:02:16,565 --> 00:02:22,040
Distributed Data Protection (Azure Storage), and Distributed Data Protection (Redis).

34
00:02:22,605 --> 00:02:27,853
Each row shows the name of the feature, its identifier, and its description.

35
00:02:27,853 --> 00:02:31,501
A recipe uses the identifier to enable the feature.

36
00:02:31,501 --> 00:02:37,005
Distributed Data Protection (Redis) also lists Redis, the feature it depends on.

37
00:02:37,478 --> 00:02:42,362
Let's enable Distributed Data Protection (Azure Storage) with its Enable button.

38
00:02:42,362 --> 00:02:45,429
A message confirms that the feature was enabled.

39
00:02:45,429 --> 00:02:49,858
Without settings, it changes nothing, and the local folder stays in use.

40
00:02:54,058 --> 00:03:01,070
The Azure settings go in the OrchardCore section of appsettings.json, under DataProtection, then Azure.

41
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:03,322
ConnectionString is required.

42
00:03:03,322 --> 00:03:08,918
Without it, the feature registers nothing and writes a critical entry to the log.

43
00:03:09,668 --> 00:03:15,716
ContainerName defaults to dataprotection and is always lowercased, and BlobName is optional.

44
00:03:15,716 --> 00:03:21,332
CreateContainer is true by default, so the container is created if it does not exist.

45
00:03:21,332 --> 00:03:24,788
Set it to false when the container already exists.

46
00:03:25,538 --> 00:03:29,862
By default, one container holds the keys of all tenants, as an XML

47
00:03:29,862 --> 00:03:32,624
file in a folder named after the tenant.

48
00:03:32,624 --> 00:03:36,888
When a tenant is removed, the feature deletes that tenant's file.

49
00:03:37,638 --> 00:03:40,148
For a container per tenant, use Liquid.

50
00:03:40,148 --> 00:03:44,945
ContainerName and BlobName are the only settings that accept a template, and the

51
00:03:44,945 --> 00:03:47,957
ShellSettings of the tenant are available in it.

52
00:03:47,957 --> 00:03:51,248
Only the default Liquid filters and tags can be used.

53
00:03:51,998 --> 00:03:55,780
The startup of the feature runs after the default registration.

54
00:03:55,780 --> 00:04:00,493
When a connection string exists, it removes the default key management setup and

55
00:04:00,493 --> 00:04:04,768
persists the keys to a blob, using the container and blob names you saw.

56
00:04:08,968 --> 00:04:13,270
Redis is configured in the OrchardCore section, under Redis.

57
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:17,507
Configuration is the connection string, and it is required.

58
00:04:17,507 --> 00:04:22,916
InstancePrefix is added to every key, so applications can share a server, and

59
00:04:22,916 --> 00:04:25,458
AllowAdmin allows admin commands.

60
00:04:26,208 --> 00:04:30,011
The key ring of each tenant is stored under one Redis key, built

61
00:04:30,011 --> 00:04:33,976
from the instance prefix, the tenant name, and DataProtection-Keys.

62
00:04:33,976 --> 00:04:37,996
The slide shows the key of the Default tenant, with the prefix MyApp

63
00:04:37,996 --> 00:04:39,408
followed by a colon.

64
00:04:40,158 --> 00:04:45,085
Data protection keys are not cache entries, so the Redis server needs AOF

65
00:04:45,085 --> 00:04:48,079
or RDB persistence, and a backup strategy.

66
00:04:48,079 --> 00:04:52,944
The feature logs a warning when persistence is not confirmed, and it can

67
00:04:52,944 --> 00:04:55,688
only check it when AllowAdmin is true.

68
00:04:56,438 --> 00:05:00,935
When Redis is configured, the feature adds a key management setup.

69
00:05:00,935 --> 00:05:05,431
It builds the Redis key of the tenant, and sets the XML repository

70
00:05:05,431 --> 00:05:09,178
of the key manager to a Redis repository for that key.

71
00:05:13,378 --> 00:05:15,772
After the site starts, read the log.

72
00:05:15,772 --> 00:05:21,186
A critical entry about a missing connection string means the Azure feature is not active.

73
00:05:21,186 --> 00:05:25,575
An error entry saying the Redis features are not active means the Redis

74
00:05:25,575 --> 00:05:27,968
configuration is missing or invalid.

75
00:05:28,718 --> 00:05:30,452
Then look in the store.

76
00:05:30,452 --> 00:05:34,518
In Azure, look for one XML file for each tenant, and in Redis,

77
00:05:34,518 --> 00:05:36,551
for one key for each tenant.

78
00:05:36,551 --> 00:05:40,258
Servers that point at the same store share the key ring.

79
00:05:42,258 --> 00:05:47,818
Shared, durable keys let every server read the cookies and saved credentials of a tenant.

80
00:05:47,818 --> 00:05:51,037
A separate video covers the other Redis features.

81
00:05:51,037 --> 00:05:52,558
Thanks for watching.

